From: dArkOS RG52 Mini port
Subject: The masked GS memory clears prefetch a kilobyte ahead

GSRendererHW::ClearGSLocalMemory clears whole pages of GS local memory. Its
masked and 24-bit loops read, mask and write back each 16 bytes, and that
memory is seldom in the cache: on the RK3562's in-order Cortex-A53 the loads
wait for DRAM (four fifths of the function's time in God of War, where it was
13% of the GS thread), as the core's prefetcher does not get far enough ahead
of the stream. The loops now go 64 bytes at a time and ask for each line a
kilobyte before they reach it (PRFM PSTL1KEEP). On an RG52 Mini, clearing a
640x448 frame out of the caches: 468 us against 617 (-24%), the same with 2 or
4 KB ahead; PSTL1STRM gave -11%, and storing the three low bytes of each
24-bit pixel without loading (ST3 lane stores) +37%. The memory written is the
same in every case. In God of War the function fell from 3.9% to 2.8% of all
samples.

